+# Fix Subagent Prompt Template
+
+Use this template when dispatching a fix round. `isolated: true` below is a
+task-tool parameter (required by the schema; fixers edit files) — pass it on
+the tool call, not inside the prompt text. Choose the agent per SKILL.md
+Agent Selection; rounds 4-5 bump the agent type one tier. `[BRIEF_FILE]` is
+the brief's repo-RELATIVE path; `[REPORT_URI]` is `local://task-N-report.md`.
+Never substitute an absolute path into either — see SKILL.md File Handoffs.
+
+```
+Subagent (task tool):
+  isolated: true
+  description: "Fix Task N round R: [task name]"
+  agent: [AGENT — per SKILL.md Agent Selection; rounds 4-5 at least one
+         tier above the agent that got stuck]
+  prompt: |
+    You are fixing review findings on Task N: [task name], fix round R.
+
+    ## The Task
+
+    Read the task brief first: [BRIEF_FILE]
+    (a path relative to your working directory)
+
+    ## The Findings
+
+    Each finding below is a defect a reviewer observed in the current code:
+
+    [FINDINGS]
+
+    ## Prior History
+
+    Read the report file — it is the memory of every prior round:
+    [REPORT_URI]
+
+    ## Covering Tests
+
+    The tests covering the amended code: [TEST_FILES]
+    Extend these files for regression tests where they already cover the
+    area; run them, not the whole suite.
+
+    ## Your Job — per finding, in this order
+
+    A review finding is a known, already-observed defect. Prove each one
+    before you fix it:
+
+    1. **RED:** Reproduce the finding as a failing test FIRST — extend an
+       existing covering test file where one exists. Do not touch the
+       production code yet.
+    2. **Run it. Watch it fail.** Confirm it fails *for the finding's
+       reason* — not a typo, an import error, or a bad assertion. A test
+       failing for the wrong reason proves nothing.
+    3. **Fix the production code minimally.**
+    4. **GREEN:** Re-run. Confirm the new test passes, the output is
+       pristine, and the neighbouring tests in the covering files still
+       pass.
+
+    Never write the test after the fix: it passes on its first run and
+    proves nothing — including when the fix is wrong.
+
+    **NOT TESTABLE escape hatch:** some findings have no observable
+    behaviour — naming, dead code, a comment, a pure refactor. For those,
+    skip the cycle and write `NOT TESTABLE: <why>` in the fix report
+    instead of RED/GREEN. Anything with observable behaviour — wrong
+    value, wrong contract, missing validation, wrong ordering — is
+    testable and gets the cycle. Do not stretch this hatch to avoid
+    writing a test.
+
+    ## You Do Not Dispatch Subagents
+
+    Do all of this round's work yourself. Never spawn a subagent to fix
+    part of it, and never spawn a reviewer to check your fix: the
+    controller dispatches a scoped re-review against your diff after you
+    report. A reviewer you spawn duplicates that review at full cost, and
+    its approval counts for nothing in the process. Report instead.
+
+    Do NOT run git or jj — you work in an isolated snapshot and the harness
+    captures your changes automatically when you finish.
+
+    Your working directory IS the project root: an isolated snapshot that
+    contains the full repo at the same relative paths. Use relative paths
+    for every file operation and every command. NEVER use an absolute path
+    into the original checkout — not in file edits, not as a bash working
+    directory — it would bypass your snapshot and corrupt the shared tree.
+    The only non-relative target you write is your local:// report.
+
+    ## Report Format
+
+    Append to [REPORT_URI] a section titled `## Fix Round R` with, per
+    finding:
+    - The finding one-liner
+    - **RED:** command run, the relevant failing output before the fix,
+      and why that failure was expected
+    - **GREEN:** command run and the relevant passing output after the fix
+    - Files changed
+    — or the `NOT TESTABLE: <why>` line with its justification.
+
+    Then report back with ONLY (under 15 lines — the detail lives in the
+    report file):
+    - **Status:** DONE | DONE_WITH_CONCERNS | BLOCKED | NEEDS_CONTEXT
+    - Files changed (paths only)
+    - One-line test summary (e.g. "3 findings: 3 RED→GREEN, 14/14 passing")
+    - Your concerns, if any
+    - The report URI
+
+    If BLOCKED or NEEDS_CONTEXT, put the specifics in the final message
+    itself — the controller acts on it directly. Never silently produce
+    work you're unsure about.
+```
+
+**Placeholders:**
+- `[AGENT]` — per SKILL.md Agent Selection; rounds 4-5 bump one tier
+- `[BRIEF_FILE]` — the task brief's repo-relative path (same file the
+  implementer worked from)
+- `[FINDINGS]` — the open Critical/Important findings and spec gaps from
+  the review, copied verbatim, one per bullet
+- `[REPORT_URI]` — the `local://task-N-report.md` URI (fix reports appended)
+- `[TEST_FILES]` — the test files covering the amended code, named by the
+  controller so the fixer runs a focused set, not the whole suite
+
+**Fixer returns:** status, files changed, a one-line test summary, concerns,
+and the report URI. The per-finding RED/GREEN evidence lives in the report
+file, not the return message.
